This follows along the work of \citet{Lassen2005} who leveraged an exogenous shock 
of an information campaign in some electoral districts and found that 
increasing voters' information also produced a sizeable effect on the propensity 
to vote. 

The 2011 election was also marked by a highly contested statewide Supreme Court 
election and the ultimate effect of Act 10 was in doubt due to multiple legal 
challenges. By the 2012 election, Act 10 had been ruled constitutional and there 
was no state supreme court seat up for election. 


Unfortunately, the present study cannot distinguish between these two mechanism. 
More to the point, the study is limited by the reality that though school boards 
were given this freedom, it came coupled with historical cuts in state aid and 
a freeze on school district revenue limits. In practice this meant that though 
compensation policy may have been freed up, school boards had very little 
flexibility to innovate under such fiscal constraint. In some sense then, this 
leaves the elections post Act 10 as really a referendum on the opinion of voters 
and communities on both the changes to collective bargaining, but also reductions 
in school funding. 

As in Chapter \ref{chap:candidacy}, I use three separate measures of contestation. 
First, I use the presence of any challenger either appearing on the ballot or receiving 
more than 10 votes as a write-in candidate. I code these races as ``minorly contested'' 
because they have a nominal level of contestation at a minimum. Next, I use a higher 
threshold where only races with challengers who receive 20 or more votes are 
considered as ``seriously contested''. Finally, I use a measure of race competitiveness, 
the Blais-Lago quotient. 

