\section{Data}

The data in this chapter is drawn from a unique collection of school board election 
results in Wisconsin school districts from 2002-2012. In addition to the election 
results from over 75\% of Wisconsin school districts, administrative records are 
incorporated to include annual measures of school districts on fiscal, political, 
demographic, and educational dimensions.

\subsection{Dependent Variables}

In this section I outline the key dependent variables for this investigation. 
Broadly, I am looking for evidence of the causal impact of state level policy 
shocks on the behavior of both candidates and voters. For candidates, I look 
for a change in the patterns of candidate contestation and seat competitiveness 
after the passage of Act 10 and the granting of additional powers to school boards. 
For voters, I look for changes in voter turnout and decreases in voter rolloff. 

<<minorContestPlot, fig.cap="Polarization and Contestation - Minor Contestation", out.height='3.18in'>>=
plotdf <- dist_turn[dist_turn$year > 2006, ]

qplot(contestMinor, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf, geom = 'boxplot') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) +
  scale_y_continuous() +
  theme_dpi(base_size=10) + coord_flip() + 
  labs(x= "Minor Contestation", y = "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall")
@


<<serContestPlot, fig.cap="Polarization and Contestation - Serious Contestation", out.height='3.18in'>>=
qplot(contestSer, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf, geom = 'boxplot') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) +
  scale_y_continuous() +
  theme_dpi(base_size=10) + coord_flip()+ 
  labs(x= "Serious Contestation", y = "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall")
@

Figures \ref{fig:minorContestPlot} and \ref{fig:serContestPlot} show the relationship 
between the community polarization in the Gubernatorial recall vote and contestation. 
There appears to be consistently little difference in the distribution of 
polarization between districts with contested and uncontested races across the 
years. 

Figure \ref{fig:blPlot} explores this same relationship but for the continuous 
measure of seat competitiveness -- the Blais-Lago quotient. Here there is a pattern 
of elevated contestation in 2011, and an increase in competitiveness, a lower 
B-L quotient, as seat polarization increases in year 2012. There is a similar 
pattern, though weaker in 2007 and 2010 as well. 

<<blPlot, fig.cap="Polarization and Seat Competitiveness">>=
plotdf <- dist_turn[dist_turn$year > 2006, ]
plotdf <- plotdf[!is.na(plotdf$turnout), ]
plotdf <- plotdf[plotdf$turnout < 0.8, ]
qplot(RecallPolarization, minBlaisLago, data = plotdf) + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + geom_smooth(method = 'lm', aes(group = 1)) +
  scale_y_continuous() +
  theme_dpi()+ 
  labs(y= "Seat Competitiveness", x = "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall")
@

Figures \ref{fig:turnoutPlot1} and \ref{fig:turnoutPlot2} show the relationship 
between polarization and school board turnout and voter rolloff. In 2011 and 2012 
there appears to be a negative relationship between increasing polarization and 
decreased turnout. For rolloff, the relationship appears to be the same across 
all the years. 

<<turnoutPlot1, fig.cap="Polarization and School Board Turnout">>=
qplot(RecallPolarization, turnout, data = plotdf, alpha = I(0.6)) + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + geom_smooth(method='lm') +
  scale_y_continuous(label=percent) +
  theme_dpi() + 
  labs(y = "School Board Turnout", x = "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall")
@

<<turnoutPlot2, fig.cap="Polarization and Spring Rolloff">>=
qplot(RecallPolarization, turnoutDiff, data = plotdf, alpha = I(0.6)) + 
  geom_smooth(method='lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(x= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", "School Board Rolloff")

# qplot(RecallPolarization, turnoutDiff/topTicketTurnoutSpring, data =plotdf) + 
#   geom_smooth(method = "lm") + 
#    facet_wrap(~year) + scale_x_continuous(label = percent) + 
#    scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
#    theme_dpi()
@

\subsection{Independent Variables}

In addition to the suite of independent variables found in Chapters \ref{chap:candidacy} 
and \ref{chap:turnout}, this chapter features some additional key dependent variables 
drawn from results of Wisconsin's historic recall election. 

Figure \ref{fig:ivPlot1} shows the relationship between recall polarization and 
other variables for the year 2012. THe relationship between fall and recall election 
turnout and polarization is virtually identical. The top right panel shows the 
relationship between recall polarization and the Democratic share of the most 
recent presidential two-party vote - aside from a handful of outliers it follows 
in the expected direction. Finally, spring election turnout is fairly similar to 
the other turnout variables, but at a lower level with about half the turnout 
on average of the other elections. 

<<ivPlot1, fig.cap="Polarization and Other Variables">>=
plotdf <- dist_turn[dist_turn$year == 2012, ]

p1 <- qplot(RecallPolarization, GovTurnout_recall, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(x= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", y="Recall Turnout")

p2 <- qplot(RecallPolarization, presTwoPartyShareDem, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(x= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", y="Pres. Two Party Dem Share")

p3 <- qplot(RecallPolarization, recentFallTurnout, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(x= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", y="Recent Fall Turnout")

p4 <- qplot(RecallPolarization, topTicketTurnoutSpring, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(x= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall",y= "Spring 2012 Turnout")

grid.arrange(p1, p2, p3, p4)
@

Figure \ref{fig:ivPlot2} explores the relationship between recall polarization 
and demographic variables to understand the ways party-polarization in the recall 
is similar to or different than partisan polarization in other elections. In the 
top left there is a strong relationship between the logged median income and 
polarization with higher polarization more strongly associated with lower income. 
Polarization appears to be consistent across the size of the Voting Age Population 
(VAP). There is a slight increase in polarization as the percentage of VAP over 
65 increases. 

<<ivPlot2, fig.cap="Polarization and Population Control Variables">>=
p1 <- qplot(median_incomeLOG, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_x_continuous(limits = c(9.5, 11.5)) + scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0, 100)) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(y= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", x= "Median Income (log)")

p2 <- qplot(VAP_adjLOG, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_x_continuous() +
   theme_dpi() + labs(y= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", x= "VAP (log)")

p3 <- qplot(PerBachelorOrAbove, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_x_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(y= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", x="Per Bach. +")


p4 <- qplot(per65o, RecallPolarization, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_x_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(y= "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall", x="% VAP 65+")

grid.arrange(p1, p2, p3, p4)
@

\subsubsection{WERC Elections}

This chapter introduces a new data source, the results of union certification 
elections. The Wisconsin Employment Relations Commission (WERC) maintains records of the 
annual recertification of unions. I use WERC records from each year following 
the passage of Act 10. After Act 10, unions in school districts had to recertify 
in waves as their existing union contracts expired. I take WERC election results 
from 2011, 2012, 2013, and 2014. After recertifying the first time, unions must 
hold an annual recertification election. I collapse the recertifcation elections 
for all unions in the district available in all years as a measure of union 
strength over time. I explore two separate measures of union strength -- the 
turnout in all union elections in the district, and turnout in teacher union 
only elections. 

Turnout is a strong proxy for union strength because such vote records are public 
record. % explain this


Figure \ref{fig:ivPlot3} shows the relationship between independent variables and 
the turnout in school district union certification elections after the passage 
of Act 10. Polarization is slightly negatively related with WERC turnout. 
Median income is slightly positively related with WERC turnout. VAP and \% of 
VAP over 65 are not really related to this. 

<<ivPlot3, fig.cap = "Polarization and WERC Elections">>=
p1 <- qplot(RecallPolarization, turnout_overall_werc, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method='lm') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi() + 
  labs(y = "WERC Election Turnout", x = "Polarization from 2012 Gov. Recall")

p2 <- qplot(median_incomeLOG, turnout_overall_werc, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method='lm') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi() + 
  scale_x_continuous(limits = c(9.5, 11.5)) +
  labs(y = "WERC Election Turnout", x = "Median Income Log")

p3 <- qplot(per65o, turnout_overall_werc, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method='lm') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) + theme_dpi() + 
  scale_x_continuous(label = percent) +
  labs(y = "WERC Election Turnout", x = "% VAP 65+")

p4 <- qplot(VAP_adjLOG, turnout_overall_werc, data = plotdf) + 
  geom_smooth(method = 'lm') + 
   facet_wrap(~year) + 
   scale_x_continuous() + scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
   theme_dpi() + labs(y= "WERC Election Turnout", x= "VAP (log)")

grid.arrange(p1, p2, p3, p4)
@


\clearpage


\section{Results}

I organize the results as follows. In the first section, I review the results 
for the models that leverage the years 2011 and 2012 as treated by a causal 
shock and I fit lagged time models and differences-in-differences estimates 
of the impact of Act 10 across five distinct measures of actualized democracy -- 
contestation, competitiveness, incumbent defeat, voter turnout, and voter 
rolloff. In the next section, I then step away from the causal framework and 
fit longitudinal models which seek to explore evidence, if any, that this causal 
impact was mediated. 

\subsection{Causal Estimates}

I provide three sets of results for each dependent variable. The first is a 
regression with a lagged dependent variable and demographic controls, the second 
is a differences in differences estimate, and the third is a difference in 
differences estimate with district level random effects.\footnote{I tested the 
models on a restricted sample with only years after 2007, but this had no 
substantive impact on the results.}

<<contestDID, eval=TRUE, results = 'asis'>>=

library(lme4)
library(optimx)
ctrl2 <- glmerControl(optimizer = "optimx", calc.derivs = FALSE, 
                     optCtrl = list(method = "nlminb", starttests = FALSE, 
                                    kkt = FALSE, maxit = 10000))
ctrl <- glmerControl(optimizer = "Nelder_Mead", calc.derivs = FALSE)

outdata <- dist_turn[, c("distid", "year", "contestMinor", "VAP_adjLOG_sq", 
                         "VAP_adjLOG", "per65o", "NonPublicPupilPer", 
                         "incumShare", "teachShareofVoters", 
                         "fallTurnout", "nraces", "schoolMillShare", "OOH_share",
                         "PerBachelorOrAbove", 
                         "PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE", "minBlaisLago", "per_white_all",
                         "GovTurnout_recall", "RecallPolarization", "locale2", 
                         "econ_disadv_per")]

preTreat <- outdata[outdata$year <= 2010,]
preTreat$year <- preTreat$year + 1
names(preTreat) <- paste(names(preTreat), "lag", sep = "_")

# Repeat 2010 data as pre-treatment for 2012
tmp <- preTreat[preTreat$year_lag == 2011,]
tmp$year_lag <- 2012
preTreat <- rbind(preTreat, tmp)
rm(tmp)

outdata <- outdata[, c("contestMinor", "distid", "year")]
outdata <- merge(outdata, preTreat, by.y = c("distid_lag", "year_lag"), 
                 by.x = c("distid", "year"))

outdata$treatment <- ifelse(outdata$year >= 2011, abs(outdata$year - 2013), 0)

lagModcontest <- glm(contestMinor ~ contestMinor_lag + treatment + 
                       VAP_adjLOG_sq_lag + 
             VAP_adjLOG_lag + per65o_lag + nraces_lag + OOH_share_lag + 
               PerBachelorOrAbove_lag +  locale2_lag, data = outdata, 
             family = "binomial")
lagModcontestSE <- get_CL_vcov(lagModcontest, outdata$distid)
lagModcontest$se <- sqrt(diag(lagModcontestSE))

DIDmodContest <- glm(contestMinor ~ contestMinor_lag * treatment + VAP_adjLOG_sq_lag + 
             VAP_adjLOG_lag + per65o_lag + nraces_lag + OOH_share_lag +  
               PerBachelorOrAbove_lag +
             locale2_lag, data = outdata, family = "binomial")
DIDmodContestSE <- get_CL_vcov(DIDmodContest, outdata$distid)
DIDmodContest$se <- sqrt(diag(DIDmodContestSE))

MermodContest <- glmer(contestMinor ~ contestMinor_lag * treatment + 
                         VAP_adjLOG_sq_lag + 
             VAP_adjLOG_lag + per65o_lag + nraces_lag + OOH_share_lag +  
               PerBachelorOrAbove_lag + locale2_lag + (1|distid), 
             data = outdata, family = "binomial", control = ctrl)

apsrtable(lagModcontest, DIDmodContest, MermodContest, se = "robust", 
          model.names = c("Lag", "Diff in Diff", "Multilevel"), 
          stars = "default", 
          caption = "Causal Estimates of Act 10 Period on Minor Contestation", 
          label = "tab:causMinorCon", notes = list(stars.note), digits = 3, 
          Sweave = FALSE)
@

